{"id":"03-nobody","audio_seconds":50.57525,"script":{"id":"03-nobody","title":"Demo 3 \u00b7 Evaluation: nobody answers","case":"The professor asks questions but no student speaks. Every student must get 'no observed participation'; the run must not invent contributions.","lines":[{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"Let's talk about evaluating language models. Suppose you build a chatbot for course questions. How would you know it is any good? Anyone? No? Alright.","start":0.8,"end":10.53},{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"One option is a human rubric. Another is to use a second model as the judge. What could go wrong with a model as a judge? Take a moment.","start":11.28,"end":19.14},{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"Okay, I'll answer it. Judges can prefer longer answers, they can prefer their own style, and they can be swayed by the position of an answer in the prompt. So we check the judge against human labels, for example with a correlation, before we trust it.","start":20.54,"end":36.2},{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"We also separate an answer that is wrong from an answer we cannot assess. If there is no evidence, the honest result is insufficient evidence, not a low score. Please read the evaluation chapter before Thursday.","start":37.08,"end":49.57}]},"registry":{"demo-professor":"Prof Demo","koh-qing-jia":"Koh Qing Jia","tasmin":"tasmin","tina":"tina","le-yong":"le yong","prof":"prof","demo-prof-lim":"Prof Lim (demo)"},"pipeline":{"language":"en","whisper_segments":[{"start":0.0,"end":3.84,"text":"Let's talk about evaluating language models."},{"start":3.84,"end":6.88,"text":"Suppose you build a chatbot for course questions."},{"start":6.88,"end":11.44,"text":"How would you know it is any good, any one, no, all right."},{"start":11.44,"end":16.12,"text":"One option is a human rubric, another is to use a second model as the judge."},{"start":16.12,"end":18.44,"text":"What could go wrong with a model as a judge?"},{"start":18.44,"end":19.96,"text":"Take a moment."},{"start":19.96,"end":22.72,"text":"Okay, I'll answer it."},{"start":22.72,"end":24.76,"text":"Judges can prefer longer answers."},{"start":24.76,"end":29.04,"text":"They can prefer their own style, and they can be swayed by the position of an answer"},{"start":29.04,"end":35.0,"text":"in the prompt, so we check the judge against human labels, for example with a correlation"},{"start":35.0,"end":37.12,"text":"before we trust it."},{"start":37.12,"end":41.32,"text":"We also separate an answer that is wrong from an answer we cannot assess."},{"start":41.32,"end":46.72,"text":"If there is no evidence, the honest result is insufficient evidence, not a low score."},{"start":46.72,"end":49.56,"text":"Please read the evaluation chapter before Thursday."}],"aligned_segments":[{"start":0.0,"end":3.84,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Let's talk about evaluating language models."},{"start":3.84,"end":6.88,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Suppose you build a chatbot for course questions."},{"start":6.88,"end":11.44,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"How would you know it is any good, any one, no, all right."},{"start":11.44,"end":16.12,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"One option is a human rubric, another is to use a second model as the judge."},{"start":16.12,"end":18.44,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"What could go wrong with a model as a judge?"},{"start":18.44,"end":19.96,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Take a moment."},{"start":19.96,"end":22.72,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Okay, I'll answer it."},{"start":22.72,"end":24.76,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Judges can prefer longer answers."},{"start":24.76,"end":29.04,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"They can prefer their own style, and they can be swayed by the position of an answer"},{"start":29.04,"end":35.0,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"in the prompt, so we check the judge against human labels, for example with a correlation"},{"start":35.0,"end":37.12,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"before we trust it."},{"start":37.12,"end":41.32,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"We also separate an answer that is wrong from an answer we cannot assess."},{"start":41.32,"end":46.72,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"If there is no evidence, the honest result is insufficient evidence, not a low score."},{"start":46.72,"end":49.56,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Please read the evaluation chapter before Thursday."}],"voice_matches":{"SPEAKER_00":{"name":"Prof Lim (demo)","status":"identified","score":0.9729118347167969,"matches":[{"person_id":"demo-prof-lim","name":"Prof Lim (demo)","score":0.9729118347167969,"same_speaker":true},{"person_id":"demo-professor","name":"Prof Demo","score":0.06608408689498901,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"tina","name":"tina","score":0.050863444805145264,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"prof","name":"prof","score":0.002039290964603424,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"tasmin","name":"tasmin","score":-0.021505635231733322,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"le-yong","name":"le yong","score":-0.03837578371167183,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"koh-qing-jia","name":"Koh Qing Jia","score":-0.09026885777711868,"same_speaker":false}]}},"speaker_identities":{"SPEAKER_00":"Prof Lim (demo)"},"segments":[{"start":0.0,"end":49.56,"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","original_speaker":"SPEAKER_00","text":"Let's talk about evaluating language models. Suppose you build a chatbot for course questions. How would you know it is any good, any one, no, all right. One option is a human rubric, another is to use a second model as the judge. What could go wrong with a model as a judge? Take a moment. Okay, I'll answer it. Judges can prefer longer answers. They can prefer their own style, and they can be swayed by the position of an answer in the prompt, so we check the judge against human labels, for example with a correlation before we trust it. We also separate an answer that is wrong from an answer we cannot assess. If there is no evidence, the honest result is insufficient evidence, not a low score. Please read the evaluation chapter before Thursday."}],"diarization_turns":[{"start":0.79034375,"end":10.797218750000003,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00"},{"start":11.185343750000001,"end":19.184093750000002,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00"},{"start":20.53409375,"end":36.26159375,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00"},{"start":37.05471875,"end":49.59284375,"speaker":"SPEAKER_00"}],"timings":{"transcription_s":8.770052873063833,"diarization_s":83.800836318871,"total_s":121.47711744299158}},"segment_matches":[[{"person_id":"demo-prof-lim","name":"Prof Lim (demo)","score":0.8983585834503174,"same_speaker":true},{"person_id":"demo-professor","name":"Prof Demo","score":0.030580244958400726,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"tina","name":"tina","score":-0.001979110762476921,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"prof","name":"prof","score":-0.010405896231532097,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"tasmin","name":"tasmin","score":-0.03458932414650917,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"le-yong","name":"le yong","score":-0.036537304520606995,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"koh-qing-jia","name":"Koh Qing Jia","score":-0.11884565651416779,"same_speaker":false}],[{"person_id":"demo-prof-lim","name":"Prof Lim (demo)","score":0.868004322052002,"same_speaker":true},{"person_id":"tina","name":"tina","score":0.05405602231621742,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"demo-professor","name":"Prof Demo","score":0.026509378105401993,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"tasmin","name":"tasmin","score":-0.027056733146309853,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"prof","name":"prof","score":-0.05686918646097183,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"le-yong","name":"le yong","score":-0.07707978785037994,"same_speaker":false},{"person_id":"koh-qing-jia","name":"Koh Qing Jia","score":-0.08871345967054367,"same_speaker":false}],[{"person_id":"demo-prof-lim","name":"Prof Lim (demo)","score":0.8230831027030945,"same_speaker":true},{"person_id":"demo-professor","name":"Prof Demo","score":0.033646002411842346,"same_speak